<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Block 6 of the syllabus (syllabus/blocks/block-06-accumulator.md) as a book chapter.
  Exercise numbers in the comments refer to the exercise arc in that file.
  Question types, as chosen for the course:
    Predict   -> fill-in-the-blank with misconception feedback (demo 1.1)
    Fill gap  -> ActiveCode with locked code (demo 2.2)
    Fix bug   -> click the buggy line (demo 3.1), then fix in ActiveCode (demo 3.2)
    Write     -> ActiveCode with hidden unit tests (demo 4.1)
  CodeLens (with and without checkpoints) is used for exposition and tracing.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-block06">
  <title>Block 6: The Accumulator Pattern</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In block 2 you saw that a variable keeps its value until you change it.
      In block 5 you saw that a loop repeats the same lines once for every
      element. This block puts the two together: a variable that
      <em>survives</em> from one pass of the loop to the next, and grows a
      little on every pass. That is the <term>accumulator pattern</term>, and
      it is behind nearly every loop that adds up, counts or multiplies.
    </p>
    <p>By the end of this chapter you can:
    <ul>
      <li><p>name the three jobs in an accumulator loop: <em>init</em>, <em>update</em> and <em>use</em>;</p></li>
      <li><p>write a running sum, a running count and a running product;</p></li>
      <li><p>count only the elements that pass a test, using an <c>if</c> inside the loop;</p></li>
      <li><p>tell what job a variable is doing: <em>stepper</em>, <em>gatherer</em> or <em>flag</em>;</p></li>
      <li><p>recognise and fix the two classic accumulator bugs: the wrong starting value, and a reset inside the loop.</p></li>
    </ul></p>
    <p>
      As in blocks 4 and 5, most exercises ask you to complete or repair the
      body of a function.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-skeleton">
    <title>Fill in the skeleton</title>
    <p>
      Adding up a list takes three lines, and each has its own job: a
      starting value set <em>once</em> before the loop begins, an update that
      happens on <em>every</em> pass through the loop, and a final read of the
      result once the loop is <em>done</em>.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 1: fill in gap, locked code (demo 2.2) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex1-sum-list">
      <title>Fill in <c>sum_list</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The function header and the final <c>return</c> are locked. Replace
          the two <c>____</c> blanks: the value <c>total</c> should start with
          before the loop, and what <c>total</c> should become on each pass.
          Leave the <c>for</c> line as it is. Then press <em>Run</em>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex1">
        <preamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
def sum_list(numbers):
]]></preamble>
        <code><![CDATA[
    total = ____
    for n in numbers:
        total = ____
]]></code>
        <postamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
    return total
]]></postamble>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([4, 1, 7]), 12, "sum_list([4, 1, 7])")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([10]), 10, "a single number")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([-2, 5, -3]), 0, "negative numbers")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([]), 0, "an empty list: the loop never runs, so this is the starting value")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      The editable region also contains the <c>for</c> line. PreTeXt can lock
      only the code <em>before</em> and <em>after</em> one editable block, so
      two blanks separated by a line of given code share one region.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-init-update-use">
    <title>Init, update, use</title>
    <p>
      Three lines, three jobs, in a fixed order:
    
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>init</alert>: one line <em>before</em> the loop sets the starting value;</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>update</alert>: one line <em>inside</em> the loop changes it on every single pass;</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>use</alert>: one line <em>after</em> the loop reads the final result.</p></li>
    </ul></p>
    <p>
      Nearly every bug in this block comes from putting one of these three
      lines in the wrong place.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 2: predict / trace table (FITB, demo 1.1), then CodeLens checkpoints -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex2-trace-sum">
      <title>Trace a running sum</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Read the program without running it.</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
total = 0
for n in [4, 1, 7]:
    total = total + n
print(total)
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>Fill in the trace table: the value of <c>total</c> after each pass, and what is printed.</p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Pass</cell><cell><c>n</c></cell><cell><c>total</c> after this pass</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>1</cell><cell>4</cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell>3</cell><cell>7</cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></cell></row>
        </tabular>
        <p>The program prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>4</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>0</strcmp><feedback><p>0 is the value <em>before</em> pass 1. After pass 1, <c>total</c> is <c>0 + 4</c>.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>5</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>1</strcmp><feedback><p>That is <c>n</c>. The update adds <c>n</c> to the <em>previous</em> total, it does not replace it.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>12</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>7</strcmp><feedback><p>That is <c>n</c>. The update adds <c>n</c> to the <em>previous</em> total, it does not replace it.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>12</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>4\s*,?\s*5\s*,?\s*12</strcmp><feedback><p><c>print</c> is <em>after</em> the loop (not indented), so it runs once, at the end.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now check your table by stepping through the program. CodeLens stops
      each time the <c>for</c> line has run and asks for <c>total</c>. The
      first stop is at the start of pass 1, each later stop comes after
      another pass, and the last stop is when the loop has finished.
    </p>
    <exercise label="b6-ex2-codelens">
      <title>Step through the running sum</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Press <em>Next</em> to step. At each question, type the current value of <c>total</c>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="codelens" label="b6-cl-trace-sum">
        <code><![CDATA[
total = 0
for n in [4, 1, 7]:
    total = total + n
print(total)
]]></code>
        <checkpoint line="2" answer-variable="globals.total">
          <prompt>The <c>for</c> line has just run. What is <c>total</c> right now?</prompt>
        </checkpoint>
      </program>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      Compare this with block 5's <c>total = i</c> exercise. This time
      <c>total</c> really accumulates, because the update line adds to the
      previous value (<c>total + n</c>) instead of throwing it away.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 3: predict (FITB) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex3-count">
      <title>Trace a running count</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
count = 0
for word in ["a", "bb", "ccc"]:
    count = count + 1
print(count)
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>It prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>3</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. Same init, update, use shape as before, but counting passes instead of adding values: <c>count</c> goes up by exactly 1 every time, whatever <c>word</c> is.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>6</strcmp><feedback><p>6 is the total number of <em>letters</em>. The update adds <c>1</c>, not the length of <c>word</c>.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>1</strcmp><feedback><p><c>count</c> is not reset inside the loop, so the 1s add up.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>(1\s*,?\s*2\s*,?\s*3)</strcmp><feedback><p><c>print</c> is after the loop, so it runs once.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>How many times does the loop run? What does each pass add?</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-roles">
    <title>Naming the job a variable is doing</title>
    <p>
      Not every variable in a loop does the same kind of work. Three roles
      cover most of the loops in this course:
    
    <dl>
      <li><title>stepper</title><p>counts through the passes, like <c>i</c> in <c>for i in range(n)</c>.</p></li>
      <li><title>gatherer</title><p>builds up a result across passes, like <c>total</c> in a running sum or <c>count</c> in a running count.</p></li>
      <li><title>flag</title><p>starts one way and can switch only once: <c>found_zero = False</c>, then perhaps <c>found_zero = True</c>, and never back.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
    <p>
      Knowing a variable's role tells you what a bug looks like. A gatherer
      that is reset partway through the loop is <em>always</em> wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      Step through this function and watch the four variables in the frame
      on the right. Notice which ones change on every pass, which change
      only sometimes, and which change at most once.
    </p>
    <program language="python" interactive="codelens" label="b6-cl-roles">
      <code><![CDATA[
def summarize(numbers):
    total = 0
    negative_count = 0
    found_zero = False
    for i in range(len(numbers)):
        total = total + numbers[i]
        if numbers[i] < 0:
            negative_count = negative_count + 1
        if numbers[i] == 0:
            found_zero = True
    return total, negative_count, found_zero

print(summarize([3, -1, 0, -4]))
]]></code>
    </program>

    <!-- Exercise 4: roles. FITB with case-insensitive answers (the syllabus has it as MCQ) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex4-roles">
      <title>Label the variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For each variable in <c>summarize</c>, type its role: <c>stepper</c>, <c>gatherer</c> or <c>flag</c>.</p>
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor"><cell>Variable</cell><cell>Role</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>i</c></cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>total</c></cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>negative_count</c></cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>found_zero</c></cell><cell><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">stepper</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp case="insensitive">gatherer</strcmp><feedback><p><c>i</c> just counts positions, 0 up to <c>len(numbers) - 1</c>. It does not collect anything.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">gatherer</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp case="insensitive">stepper</strcmp><feedback><p><c>total</c> goes up by different amounts, and it collects a running sum.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">gatherer</strcmp><feedback><p>Right: a running count, updated only on some passes.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp case="insensitive">flag</strcmp><feedback><p>A flag can switch only once. <c>negative_count</c> can go up many times.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp case="insensitive">stepper</strcmp><feedback><p>A stepper moves on every pass. <c>negative_count</c> changes only when the number is negative.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">flag</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp case="insensitive">gatherer</strcmp><feedback><p>Once <c>found_zero</c> is <c>True</c>, nothing ever sets it back. It records <em>whether</em>, not <em>how many</em>.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-seed">
    <title>Choosing the right starting value</title>
    <p>
      The starting value has to fit the update. <c>0</c> is the right start
      for a running <em>sum</em>, because adding 0 changes nothing. For a
      running <em>product</em>, <c>0</c> is a disaster: multiplying by 0 wipes
      out everything, for good.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 5: predict (FITB) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex5-product">
      <title>A product that is always zero</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def product_of_list(numbers):
    total = 0
    for n in numbers:
        total = total * n
    return total

print(product_of_list([2, 3, 4]))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>It prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>0</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. On the first pass <c>total * n</c> is <c>0 * 2</c>, so <c>total</c> stays 0, and it stays 0 for every remaining number.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>24</strcmp><feedback><p>24 is what the function is <em>meant</em> to return. Trace it: what is <c>total</c> after the first pass, starting from 0?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>9</strcmp><feedback><p>That is the sum. The update multiplies.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Work out <c>total</c> after the first pass: <c>0 * 2</c>.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 6: fix bug = find (demo 3.1) + fix (demo 3.2) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex6-find">
      <title>Fix the seed, step 1: find the bug</title>
      <statement>
        <p><c>product_of_list([2, 3, 4])</c> should return <c>24</c>, but returns <c>0</c>. Click the line that causes the bug.</p>
      </statement>
      <areas language="python">
        <cline><area correct="no">def product_of_list(numbers):</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="yes">total = 0</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">for n in numbers:</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="no">total = total * n</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">return total</area></cline>
      </areas>
      <feedback>
        <p>The update line is fine: a product <em>should</em> multiply. The problem is what it multiplies first.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex6-fix">
      <title>Fix the seed, step 2: fix it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Fix the bug by changing <alert>one line only</alert>. Press <em>Run</em> to run the tests. Use <em>Reset</em> to start again.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex6">
        <code><![CDATA[
def product_of_list(numbers):
    total = 0
    for n in numbers:
        total = total * n
    return total

print(product_of_list([2, 3, 4]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(product_of_list([2, 3, 4]), 24, "product_of_list([2, 3, 4])")
        self.assertEqual(product_of_list([5]), 5, "a single number")
        self.assertEqual(product_of_list([-2, 3]), -6, "a negative number")
        self.assertEqual(product_of_list([7, 0, 2]), 0, "a zero in the list")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      Multiplying by 1 leaves a number unchanged, so <c>1</c> is the
      <q>do-nothing</q> start for a product, just as <c>0</c> is the
      do-nothing start for a sum.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-reset">
    <title>Resetting inside the loop</title>
    <p>
      If the init line ends up <em>inside</em> the loop body instead of just
      above it, the accumulator is wiped out and restarted on every single
      pass. When the loop ends, it remembers only the last pass.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 7: predict (FITB) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex7-reset">
      <title>Reset on every pass</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def sum_list_broken(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        total = 0
        total = total + n
    return total

print(sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>It prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>7</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <c>total = 0</c> is inside the loop, so every pass starts again from 0 and adds only the current element. Only the last one, 7, survives.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>12</strcmp><feedback><p>12 is what a <em>correct</em> sum would give. Look at where <c>total = 0</c> is indented: how often does it run?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>0</strcmp><feedback><p><c>total</c> is set to 0, but the very next line adds <c>n</c> before the pass ends.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>4</strcmp><feedback><p>4 is the <em>first</em> element. Which pass runs last?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Trace one pass: what does <c>total</c> hold at the end of it?</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Watch the reset happen. CodeLens stops each time the update line
      <c>total = total + n</c> has just run, and asks for <c>total</c>. If
      the sum were accumulating, you would see 4, 5, 12.
    </p>
    <exercise label="b6-ex7-codelens">
      <title>Watch <c>total</c> being reset</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Press <em>Next</em> to step, and answer each question.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="codelens" label="b6-cl-reset">
        <code><![CDATA[
def sum_list_broken(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        total = 0
        total = total + n
    return total

print(sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        <checkpoint line="4" answer-variable="current_frame.total">
          <prompt>The line <c>total = total + n</c> has just run. What is <c>total</c> now?</prompt>
          <feedback>Just before this line, <c>total = 0</c> ran. So <c>total</c> is now 0 plus the current <c>n</c>.</feedback>
        </checkpoint>
        <checkpoint line="5" answer-variable="current_frame.total">
          <prompt>The loop is over. What does the function return?</prompt>
        </checkpoint>
      </program>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 8: fix bug, find + fix -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex8-find">
      <title>Fix the placement, step 1: find the bug</title>
      <statement>
        <p><c>sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7])</c> should return <c>12</c>. Click the line that is in the wrong place.</p>
      </statement>
      <areas language="python">
        <cline><area correct="no">def sum_list_broken(numbers):</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">for n in numbers:</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="yes">total = 0</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="no">total = total + n</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">return total</area></cline>
      </areas>
      <feedback>
        <p>The init line belongs <em>before</em> the loop, so that it runs once.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex8-fix">
      <title>Fix the placement, step 2: move it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Fix the function by <alert>moving one line</alert>. Do not add or delete any other line.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex8">
        <code><![CDATA[
def sum_list_broken(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        total = 0
        total = total + n
    return total

print(sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7]), 12, "sum_list_broken([4, 1, 7])")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list_broken([7, 1, 4]), 12, "the same numbers in another order")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list_broken([]), 0, "an empty list")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 9: predict (FITB), then find + fix -->
    <p>
      Here is the same bug again, this time combined with an <c>if</c>. It
      is adapted from the course's <c>homework_6</c> notebook.
    </p>
    <exercise label="b6-ex9-predict">
      <title>The same bug, for real: predict</title>
      <statement>
        <p><c>positives</c> should count the numbers that are zero or greater, and return <c>3</c> for this list.</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def positives(lst):
    for elem in lst:
        npos = 0
        if elem >= 0:
            npos += 1
    return npos

myList = [3, -4, -7, 8, 1]
print(positives(myList))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>What does it <em>actually</em> print? <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>1</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. Every pass resets <c>npos</c> to 0 before maybe adding 1, so the result only says whether the <em>last</em> element (1) is positive.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>3</strcmp><feedback><p>3 is what it <em>should</em> print. Where is <c>npos = 0</c>?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>0</strcmp><feedback><p>The last pass resets <c>npos</c> to 0, but then checks <c>elem &gt;= 0</c> for the last element. Is 1 positive?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Trace only the last pass: <c>elem</c> is 1.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex9-find">
      <title>The same bug, for real: find it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Click the line that is in the wrong place.</p>
      </statement>
      <areas language="python">
        <cline><area correct="no">def positives(lst):</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">for elem in lst:</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="yes">npos = 0</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="no">if elem &gt;= 0:</area></cline>
        <cline>            <area correct="no">npos += 1</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">return npos</area></cline>
      </areas>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex9-fix">
      <title>The same bug, for real: fix it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Fix <c>positives</c> by <alert>moving one line</alert>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex9">
        <code><![CDATA[
def positives(lst):
    for elem in lst:
        npos = 0
        if elem >= 0:
            npos += 1
    return npos

myList = [3, -4, -7, 8, 1]
print(positives(myList))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(positives([3, -4, -7, 8, 1]), 3, "positives([3, -4, -7, 8, 1])")
        self.assertEqual(positives([5, 6, -1]), 2, "positives([5, 6, -1])")
        self.assertEqual(positives([0, -1]), 1, "zero counts as positive here")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-count-if">
    <title>Counting only when a condition is met</title>
    <p>
      To count <em>how many</em> elements pass a test, the loop still visits
      every element, but the update only runs when an <c>if</c> lets it.
      The loop running and the count changing are two different things.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 10: predict (FITB) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex10-negatives">
      <title>Count the negatives</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
count = 0
for n in [3, -1, 4, -2, 0]:
    if n < 0:
        count = count + 1
print(count)
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>It prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="5"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>2</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The loop visits all five numbers, but <c>count</c> goes up only for <c>-1</c> and <c>-2</c>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>5</strcmp><feedback><p>The loop runs 5 times, but the update is inside the <c>if</c>.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>3</strcmp><feedback><p>Is 0 less than 0?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>-3</strcmp><feedback><p>That is the <em>sum</em> of the negatives. The update adds 1, not <c>n</c>.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>For which values of <c>n</c> is <c>n &lt; 0</c> true?</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 11: write (demo 4.1) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex11-write">
      <title>Write your own count-if functions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Complete both functions:
        <ul>
          <li><p><c>count_negatives(numbers)</c> returns how many numbers in the list are negative.</p></li>
          <li><p><c>count_matching(words, letter)</c> returns how many strings in <c>words</c> start with <c>letter</c>. (Hint: a string <c>s</c> starts with <c>letter</c> when <c>s[0] == letter</c>.)</p></li>
        </ul></p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex11">
        <code><![CDATA[
def count_negatives(numbers):
    # your code here
    pass


def count_matching(words, letter):
    # your code here
    pass


print(count_negatives([3, -1, 4, -2, 0]))
print(count_matching(["apple", "banana", "avocado"], "a"))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_count_negatives(self):
        self.assertEqual(count_negatives([3, -1, 4, -2, 0]), 2, "count_negatives([3, -1, 4, -2, 0])")
        self.assertEqual(count_negatives([1, 2, 3]), 0, "no negatives")
        self.assertEqual(count_negatives([]), 0, "an empty list")
    def test_count_matching(self):
        self.assertEqual(count_matching(["apple", "banana", "avocado"], "a"), 2, "words starting with 'a'")
        self.assertEqual(count_matching(["cat", "dog"], "z"), 0, "no matches")
        self.assertEqual(count_matching(["Bob", "bob"], "b"), 1, "'B' and 'b' are different letters")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-error">
    <title>An error from a missing init</title>
    <p>
      In block 2 you met <c>NameError</c>: Python does not know a name,
      usually because of a typo. The same kind of error appears when an
      accumulator is used before its init line has run, because that line
      was deleted, or ended up after the loop instead of before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Inside a function, Python gives this error a more specific name.
      Because <c>total</c> is assigned somewhere in the function, Python
      treats it as the function's own (local) variable, so reading it before
      it has a value raises <c>UnboundLocalError</c>, a close cousin of
      <c>NameError</c>. Either way, the cause is the same: the name has no
      value yet at that point in the program. (The message after the error
      name differs between Python versions.)
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 12: predict an error (FITB) -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex12-missing-init">
      <title>Missing init entirely</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def sum_list_missing_init(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        total = total + n
    return total

print(sum_list_missing_init([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
          Does this run? If it prints a number, type the number. If it stops
          with an error, type only the name of the error.
        </p>
        <p><fillin mode="string" characters="20"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>UnboundLocalError(:.*)?</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Yes. On the very first pass, <c>total + n</c> tries to read <c>total</c>, which has no value yet.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test><strcmp>NameError(:.*)?</strcmp><feedback><p>Very close, and the cause is exactly that: <c>total</c> has no value yet. Inside a function, Python reports this as the more specific <c>UnboundLocalError</c>.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>12</strcmp><feedback><p>That would need <c>total</c> to start at 0. Where is <c>total</c> given its first value?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*Error.*</strcmp><feedback><p>It is an error, but not that one. What is wrong with <c>total + n</c> on the first pass?</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>What is <c>total</c> the first time <c>total + n</c> runs?</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 13: fix bug. The "find" step locates where the error is reported,
         which is not the line that needs changing. -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex13-find">
      <title>Add back the missing line, step 1: where does it crash?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Click the line where Python stops with the error.</p>
      </statement>
      <areas language="python">
        <cline><area correct="no">def sum_list_missing_init(numbers):</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">for n in numbers:</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="yes">total = total + n</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">return total</area></cline>
      </areas>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          The error is <em>reported</em> on the update line, because that is
          where <c>total</c> is first read. But that line is correct. The fix
          is a line that is missing further up. The line in the error message
          is where Python noticed the problem, not always where it is.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex13-fix">
      <title>Add back the missing line, step 2: fix it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Fix the function by <alert>adding one line</alert>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex13">
        <code><![CDATA[
def sum_list_missing_init(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        total = total + n
    return total

print(sum_list_missing_init([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(sum_list_missing_init([4, 1, 7]), 12, "sum_list_missing_init([4, 1, 7])")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list_missing_init([]), 0, "an empty list")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="b6-write">
    <title>Write your own</title>
    <p>
      Write each function from scratch, with the full init, update, use
      structure. There are no blanks this time.
    </p>

    <!-- Exercise 14: write (demo 4.1), three parts -->
    <exercise label="b6-ex14-sum">
      <title><c>sum_list</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Write <c>sum_list(numbers)</c>, which returns the sum of all the numbers. Do not use Python's built-in <c>sum()</c>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex14-sum">
        <code><![CDATA[
# write sum_list here


print(sum_list([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

def _banned(*args, **kwargs):
    raise Exception("sum() is not allowed in this exercise: write the loop yourself")
sum = _banned

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([4, 1, 7]), 12, "sum_list([4, 1, 7])")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([-5, 5]), 0, "sum_list([-5, 5])")
        self.assertEqual(sum_list([]), 0, "an empty list")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex14-average">
      <title><c>average</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Write <c>average(numbers)</c>, which returns the average of a non-empty list: the sum divided by the count. Use an accumulator for each.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex14-average">
        <code><![CDATA[
# write average here


print(average([4, 1, 7]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(average([4, 1, 7]), 4, "average([4, 1, 7])")
        self.assertEqual(average([1, 2, 3, 4]), 2.5, "average([1, 2, 3, 4])")
        self.assertEqual(average([-3]), -3, "a single number")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="b6-ex14-count">
      <title><c>count_matching</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Write <c>count_matching(numbers, threshold)</c>, which returns how many numbers in the list are <em>greater than</em> <c>threshold</c>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="b6-ac-ex14-count">
        <code><![CDATA[
# write count_matching here


print(count_matching([4, 1, 7, 5], 4))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(count_matching([4, 1, 7, 5], 4), 2, "count_matching([4, 1, 7, 5], 4): 4 itself is not greater than 4")
        self.assertEqual(count_matching([1, 2], 10), 0, "nothing is greater")
        self.assertEqual(count_matching([-1, -2, 0], -3), 3, "everything is greater")
        self.assertEqual(count_matching([], 0), 0, "an empty list")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
